.TH BTRFS-SNAPMAN 1 "November 2026" "btrfs-snapman 0.6.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
btrfs-snapman \- GTK4 manager for btrfs subvolume snapshots
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B btrfs-snapman
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B btrfs-snapman
lists the volumes defined in
.I /etc/btrfs-snapman
and the snapshots in each volume's snapshot folder.
It can create and delete snapshots, toggle their read-only flag, and create or
remove volumes.
New volumes go under a parent folder: one listed in the config, or one chosen
with
.B Browse
in the New volume dialog and approved as a new location.
.PP
The GUI runs as the calling user.
Every operation goes through
.BR pkexec (1)
to a root helper in
.IR /usr/libexec/btrfs-snapman ,
each governed by its own polkit action:
listing needs no password for the active local session, snapshot changes ask
for an administrator password (cached for a few minutes), and creating or
removing a volume, or approving a new location, asks every time.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/btrfs-snapman/config.toml
Shared settings, hand-defined volumes and the parents under which new volumes
may be created.
Must be owned by root and not group- or world-writable.
The program never modifies it.
.TP
.I /etc/btrfs-snapman/volumes.d/<name>.toml
One file per volume created in the GUI.
.TP
.I /etc/btrfs-snapman/parents.d/<path>.toml
One file per parent folder approved in the GUI, named after its path
(e.g.\&
.IR mnt-vmstorage-vmdisks.toml ).
.TP
.I /usr/share/doc/btrfs-snapman/examples/config.example.toml
Annotated example configuration.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR btrfs-subvolume (8),
.BR pkexec (1),
.BR journalctl (1)
.PP
Changes are logged to the journal: \fBjournalctl \-t btrfs-snapman\fR.
